r-field/type :answer-field.type/checkbox :answer-field/choices ["da8bf321-361b-55da-a675-b901506bcb36" "de02c92b-35bb-5677-9dbc-e91148ece4fe"] :answer-field/correct "da8bf321-361b-55da-a675-b901506bcb36" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "2d36471b-5787-521f-824c-19b0424cab8b" :question/id #uuid "2d36471b-5787-521f-824c-19b0424cab8b" :question/problem "![](images/86/86c9b7da8fbc76a10fde6f35b50138b5cd53aae18e204be5e6e6f58562367183.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nI. $x\\in (-2,0]$\n\nII. $x\\in [0,2]$\n\nIII. $x\\in [-1,2]$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- On interval I, $x\\in (-2,0]$, the function is invertible. Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the restricted graph at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- On interval II, $x\\in [0,2]$, the function is invertible. Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the restricted graph at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- On interval III, $x\\in [-1,2]$, the function is **not** invertible. Some horizontal lines will intersect the restricted graph more than once.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["2468fc43-bb2f-51c8-afce-10350b91c677" "2d203ee5-9363-5c83-be78-461d7c935da1" "0cf2f0eb-c62b-5ce1-ab6a-bf06fb4db508"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "d42bb9f1-594b-5b76-a75c-9c7769e94c9f" :answer/id #uuid "d42bb9f1-594b-5b76-a75c-9c7769e94c9f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "false" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "1996ca96-b5de-53b0-8b79-7ffa16a4974f" :answer/id #uuid "1996ca96-b5de-53b0-8b79-7ffa16a4974f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "true" :db/ensure :answer/val